window.CHAPTER={"n":4,"title":"Rules of the Air","tag":"Annex 2 · right of way · lights · flight plans · RCF · VMC · signals · cruising levels","icon":"plane","hue":3,"partOfBook":"Part I · Air Regulations","big":"The everyday rules of flying: who gives way, which lights you show, when and how to file, what to do when the radio dies, how much weather VFR needs, every signal from the ground or an interceptor, and which level to cruise at.","purpose":"The largest chapter of the paper: right-of-way, nav-light geometry (110/110/140), flight plan timings (3 h, 120 h, 15 min), RCF (20 min, 7 min, 30 min), the VMC table, light signals, ground signals, interception series and the semicircular rule are asked every session.","lessons":[{"id":"map","type":"mindmap","short":"Mind map","icon":"brain","ask":"The whole chapter on one page","root":{"label":"Rules of the Air","children":[{"label":"General","children":[{"label":"PIC final authority"},{"label":"Aerobatics done by 2,000 ft"},{"label":"No formation, no towing (India)"}]},{"label":"Right of way","children":[{"label":"Head-on: both right"},{"label":"Converging: other on right gives way"},{"label":"Overtaking: < 70°, go right"},{"label":"Lower lands first"}]},{"label":"Lights","children":[{"label":"Red L 110°"},{"label":"Green R 110°"},{"label":"White tail 140°"}]},{"label":"Flight plan","children":[{"label":"≥ 3 h, ≤ 120 h"},{"label":"Delay 15 min"},{"label":"Close on arrival"}]},{"label":"Comms failure","children":[{"label":"VMC: land nearest"},{"label":"Non-radar 20 min"},{"label":"Radar 7 min"},{"label":"Land ≤ 30 min after ETA/EAT"}]},{"label":"VFR / IFR","children":[{"label":"VMC 8 km / 5 km"},{"label":"SVFR 1,500 m"},{"label":"IFR 2,000 / 1,000 ft in 8 km"}]},{"label":"Signals","children":[{"label":"Distress & urgency"},{"label":"Interception series 1–6"},{"label":"Tower lights"},{"label":"Ground panels"},{"label":"Marshalling"}]},{"label":"Levels","children":[{"label":"000–179 odd"},{"label":"180–359 even"},{"label":"VFR +500"},{"label":"RVSM 290–410"}]}]}},{"id":"story","short":"Whose rules","takeaway":"The rules apply over India and to Indian aircraft everywhere. The PIC answers for them whether flying or not, has final authority, and may break them only when safety demands it.","type":"story","icon":"plane","ask":"Start here: whose rules, and who is in charge?","steps":[{"kicker":"Where they apply","title":"Over India, and on every VT aircraft","icon":"globe","text":["The rules apply to **all aircraft over Indian territory** and to **Indian-registered aircraft wherever they are**, unless they clash with the rules of the State being overflown.","Over the high seas, Annex 2 applies unchanged."]},{"kicker":"Who answers","title":"The pilot-in-command","icon":"user","text":["The PIC is responsible **whether or not touching the controls**, and has **final authority** over the aircraft.","The PIC may break a rule only when it is **absolutely necessary for safety**."]},{"kicker":"Before you go","title":"Pre-flight action","icon":"doc","text":["Know all the information for the flight. For flights away from the aerodrome and **all IFR flights**: current weather and forecasts, fuel, and a **plan B** if the flight cannot be completed."]},{"kicker":"Always","title":"General rules + VFR or IFR","icon":"list","text":["Every flight obeys the **general rules**, plus either the **visual** or the **instrument** flight rules."]}]},{"id":"aero","short":"Prohibited flying","part":"General rules","type":"bullets","icon":"alert","ask":"What may you not do with an aircraft in India?","dgca":true,"takeaway":"No negligent or reckless flying. Aerobatics only by a licensed pilot, with passengers' written consent, finishing by 2,000 ft AGL and never within 2 NM of an aerodrome below 6,000 ft. No formation flying and no towing of aircraft by civil aircraft in India. Drops, sprays and parachute descents only as permitted.","hook":"Aerobatics: 2 NM, 2,000 ft, written consent.","groups":[{"title":"Aerobatics","icon":"rotate","points":["Licensed pilot, or a trainee with an instructor","Passengers' **written** consent; all belted in","Complete by **600 m (2,000 ft) AGL** or higher if the C of A says","Not within **2 NM** of an aerodrome perimeter unless above **1,800 m (6,000 ft)**","Not over towns, villages or crowds (unless promoters applied in writing to DG)"]},{"title":"Formation","icon":"plane","points":["**Not allowed** for civil aircraft in India","ICAO: by pre-arrangement; acts as **one aircraft**","Within **1 km (0.5 NM)** laterally/longitudinally and **30 m (100 ft)** vertically of the leader"]},{"title":"Towing, drops, parachutes","icon":"weight","points":["No aircraft tows an aircraft; **towing of aircraft not permitted in India**","Dropping/spraying under **Rule 26**, Aircraft Rules 1937, and ATS clearance","Parachute descents only in emergency or by Central Government order"]},{"title":"Heights","icon":"mountain","points":["Over congested areas: high enough to land safely after an emergency","Flight levels above the TA, altitudes at or below it"]}]},{"id":"row","short":"Who gives way","part":"Right of way","type":"svg","fig":"reg-right-of-way","icon":"route","ask":"Who gives way?","dgca":true,"takeaway":"Head-on: both turn right. Converging: the aircraft that has the other on its right gives way. Overtaking: the one behind (within 70° of the other's tail) keeps clear by turning right. The aircraft with right of way keeps its heading and speed.","hook":"If it's on your right, it's in the right."},{"id":"row-cats","short":"Give-way ladder","type":"layers","icon":"layers","ask":"Who gives way to whom when converging?","dgca":true,"takeaway":"Least manoeuvrable wins: power-driven heavier-than-air aircraft give way to airships, gliders and balloons; airships give way to gliders and balloons; gliders give way to balloons. Any power-driven aircraft gives way to one seen to be towing.","hook":"Balloons beat gliders beat airships beat powered planes.","bands":[{"label":"Balloon","sub":"gives way to nobody","h":54,"tip":"Cannot steer at all, so everyone gives way to it.","points":["Least manoeuvrable of all"]},{"label":"Glider","sub":"gives way to balloons","h":54,"tip":"No engine to climb away; gives way only to balloons.","points":["Powered aircraft give way to it, even when landing"]},{"label":"Airship","sub":"gives way to gliders, balloons","h":54,"points":["Power-driven but lighter than air"]},{"label":"Aircraft towing","sub":"priority over powered aircraft","h":54,"tip":"Power-driven aircraft give way to aircraft seen to be towing other aircraft or objects.","points":["Towing a glider, banner or other object"]},{"label":"Power-driven heavier-than-air","sub":"gives way to all above","h":54,"tip":"Aeroplanes and helicopters. Between two of these, the one with the other on its right gives way.","points":["Aeroplanes and helicopters","Between two: other on your right → you give way"]}]},{"id":"row-stamp","short":"You or them","takeaway":"If the other aircraft is on your right, you give way; head-on, both turn right; the overtaker keeps clear; the lower landing aircraft and anything already landing have priority.","type":"stamp","icon":"check","ask":"You or them: who gives way?","stamps":[{"id":"me","label":"I give way"},{"id":"they","label":"They give way"},{"id":"both","label":"Both turn right"}],"cases":[{"text":"You (aeroplane) see another aeroplane converging from your right at the same level.","answer":"me","why":"It is on your right, so you give way."},{"text":"You (aeroplane) see another aeroplane converging from your left.","answer":"they","why":"You are on its right; it gives way. You hold heading and speed."},{"text":"An aeroplane is approaching you head-on.","answer":"both"},{"text":"You (aeroplane) converge with a glider on your left.","answer":"me","why":"Power-driven aircraft give way to gliders regardless of side."},{"text":"You (glider) converge with an airship on your right.","answer":"they","why":"Airships give way to gliders."},{"text":"You are overtaking a slower aircraft from behind.","answer":"me","why":"The overtaking aircraft keeps clear by altering to the right."},{"text":"You are on final; another aircraft is taxiing to the runway.","answer":"they","why":"Aircraft on the ground give way to aircraft landing or on final."},{"text":"You (aeroplane) are on approach above another aeroplane also landing.","answer":"me","why":"The higher aircraft gives way to the lower one."}]},{"id":"row-land","short":"Landing and taxiing","takeaway":"Of two landing aircraft the lower goes first, but powered aircraft always give way to gliders. Taxiing aircraft give way to those taking off. On the ground, head-on means stop or turn right.","type":"pairs","icon":"plane","ask":"Landing, taking off and taxiing","dgca":true,"pairs":[{"a":{"label":"Two aircraft landing","body":"The **higher gives way to the lower**, but the lower must not cut in front of one on final or overtake it."},"b":{"label":"Gliders","body":"Power-driven heavier-than-air aircraft give way to **gliders** even when landing."},"rule":"Lower first, glider first."},{"a":{"label":"Take-off","body":"Aircraft **taxiing** on the manoeuvring area give way to aircraft **taking off or about to**."},"b":{"label":"Emergency landing","body":"Give way to any aircraft known to be **compelled to land**."},"rule":""},{"a":{"label":"Taxiing head-on","body":"**Stop**, or where practicable turn **right**."},"b":{"label":"Taxiing converging","body":"The one with the other **on its right** gives way. The overtaken one has right of way."},"rule":"Stop at every runway-holding position and every lit stop bar unless cleared."}]},{"id":"navlights","short":"Navigation lights","part":"Lights","type":"svg","fig":"reg-nav-lights","icon":"zap","ask":"What do the navigation lights tell you?","dgca":true,"takeaway":"Red on the left wing and green on the right each shine 110° from dead ahead; the white tail light covers 140° (70° each side of the tail). A light holding a steady bearing means collision risk: see green and you have right of way; see red and you give way by turning right.","hook":"Red to red or green to green: safe. Red and green together: head-on."},{"id":"lights-stamp","short":"Read the lights","takeaway":"Red and green together means head-on, white alone means it is going away, red and white means it crosses right to left, green and white left to right.","type":"stamp","icon":"eye","ask":"At night you see… where is it going?","stamps":[{"id":"head","label":"Coming at me"},{"id":"away","label":"Going away"},{"id":"rl","label":"Crossing right → left"},{"id":"lr","label":"Crossing left → right"}],"cases":[{"text":"A red light and a green light together.","answer":"head"},{"text":"Only a white light.","answer":"away"},{"text":"A red light and a white light.","answer":"rl","why":"You see its port (left) side, so it is moving from your right to your left."},{"text":"A green light and a white light.","answer":"lr","why":"You see its starboard side: it moves left to right."}]},{"id":"lights-when","short":"Which lights when","takeaway":"From sunset to sunrise show navigation and anti-collision lights in flight; aircraft fitted with anti-collision lights show them by day too. On the ground also show lights for extremities and running engines.","type":"matrix","icon":"clock","ask":"Which lights, and when?","revise":true,"tier":"exam","corner":"Situation","cols":["Lights"],"rows":[{"label":"In flight, sunset–sunrise","cells":[{"v":"anti-collision + navigation lights","key":true}]},{"label":"Movement area, at night","cells":["nav lights; lights at the extremities (unless lit); attention lights; engines-running lights"]},{"label":"Fitted with anti-collision lights","cells":[{"v":"show them by day too, in flight and on the movement area","key":true}]},{"label":"Dazzle or distraction","cells":["pilot may switch off or dim flashing lights"]},{"label":"Extra","cells":["landing lights and floodlights may be added for conspicuity"]}]},{"id":"aerodrome","short":"Near an aerodrome","takeaway":"Near an aerodrome watch the traffic, make left turns unless told otherwise, and take off and land into wind. With a tower, keep a listening watch and get permission before moving.","type":"bullets","icon":"tower","ask":"Flying at or near an aerodrome","groups":[{"title":"Always","icon":"eye","points":["Watch other traffic","Join or avoid the pattern","**Turns to the left** after take-off and before landing unless told otherwise","Land and take off **into wind** unless safety, runway or traffic dictates"]},{"title":"With a tower","icon":"radio","points":["**Continuous listening watch** on tower frequency (or watch for light signals)","Prior authorisation for any taxi, take-off or landing manoeuvre"]},{"title":"Simulated IF","icon":"gauge","points":["Fully working **dual controls**","A qualified **safety pilot** in a control seat, with good forward and side view (or an observer helping)"]}]},{"id":"fpl","short":"Filing times","part":"Flight plans and clearances","type":"timeline","icon":"clock","ask":"When must a flight plan be filed?","dgca":true,"takeaway":"File at least 3 hours (180 min) and no more than 120 hours before EOBT; RPA plans at least 24 hours before. If departure slips by 15 minutes or more, tell ATC. Close the plan with an arrival report as soon as possible after landing.","hook":"Three hours before, five days at most, fifteen minutes late = call.","axisLabel":"Hours before EOBT (not to scale near zero)","min":0,"max":130,"ticks":[0,3,24,120],"marks":[{"at":120,"label":"Earliest filing","atLabel":"120 h before","tip":"A flight plan may not be filed more than 120 hours before EOBT."},{"at":24,"label":"RPA deadline","atLabel":"24 h before","tip":"Remotely piloted aircraft file at least 24 hours before departure."},{"at":3,"label":"Latest filing","atLabel":"3 h before","tip":"At least 180 minutes before EOBT, except for operational or technical reasons."},{"at":0,"label":"EOBT","atLabel":"delay ≥ 15 min → tell ATC","tip":"A delay of 15 minutes or more means notifying the revised time, amending or refiling."}]},{"id":"fpl-when","short":"Plan required?","takeaway":"A flight plan is needed for any flight receiving ATC, IFR in advisory airspace, flights across borders, and where the ATS authority requires it for SAR or air defence.","type":"classify","icon":"doc","ask":"Is a flight plan required?","dgca":true,"buckets":[{"id":"y","label":"Flight plan required"},{"id":"n","label":"Not needed"}],"items":[{"text":"Any flight to receive air traffic control service","bucket":"y"},{"text":"An IFR flight in advisory airspace","bucket":"y"},{"text":"Any flight crossing an international border","bucket":"y"},{"text":"A flight in an area where the ATS authority requires it for SAR or alerting","bucket":"y"},{"text":"A flight where it is required to avoid interception by the military","bucket":"y"},{"text":"A nano RPA below 50 ft AGL in uncontrolled airspace","bucket":"n","why":"Nano (≤ 50 ft) and micro (≤ 200 ft) RPA in uncontrolled airspace or enclosed premises are exempt."}]},{"id":"fpl-changes","short":"Changes to report","takeaway":"Regain track at once if off it; report any change to assigned Mach or IAS immediately, a TAS change of 10 kt or M 0.02, and an estimate change of more than 2 minutes.","type":"matrix","icon":"refresh","ask":"Changes you must report","revise":true,"dgca":true,"corner":"Change","cols":["Action"],"rows":[{"label":"Off track","cells":["regain track forthwith"]},{"label":"Not at ATC-assigned Mach/IAS","cells":[{"v":"inform ATS immediately","key":true}]},{"label":"Cruise Mach/TAS off plan","cells":[{"v":"± M 0.02 or ± 10 kt (19 km/h) TAS → inform ATS","key":true}]},{"label":"Time estimate changes","cells":[{"v":"more than 2 minutes → notify (auto with ADS-C)","key":true}]},{"label":"Endurance / POB wrong at departure","cells":["a significant change: report it"]},{"label":"IFR or controlled VFR","cells":["report all changes"]},{"label":"Other VFR","cells":["report significant changes"]}]},{"id":"vmc-lost","short":"Losing VMC","takeaway":"A controlled VFR flight losing VMC asks for a clearance that keeps it VMC, or stays VMC and tells ATC it is leaving or landing, or asks for special VFR in a CTR, or asks for IFR.","type":"flow","icon":"help","ask":"Controlled VFR flight: the weather is going below VMC","start":"q1","nodes":{"q1":{"text":"Can you get an amended clearance to stay in VMC to destination, an alternate, or out of controlled airspace?","yes":"e1","no":"q2"},"q2":{"text":"Are you inside a control zone?","yes":"e2","no":"q3"},"q3":{"text":"Are you rated and equipped to fly IFR?","yes":"e3","no":"e4"},"e1":{"end":true,"text":"Fly the amended VMC clearance","hue":1},"e2":{"end":true,"text":"Request special VFR","body":"Ground visibility must be at least 1,500 m (1,000 m for PC 1/2 helicopters).","hue":3},"e3":{"end":true,"text":"Request an IFR clearance","hue":0},"e4":{"end":true,"text":"Stay VMC and tell ATC","body":"Continue in VMC and notify ATC that you are leaving the airspace or landing at the nearest suitable aerodrome.","hue":4}}},{"id":"rcf","short":"Radio failure","part":"Radio failure","type":"sequence","icon":"radio","ask":"Radio failure in IMC: what do you fly?","dgca":true,"takeaway":"Squawk 7600. Hold the last assigned speed and level (or MFA if higher) for 20 minutes after a missed compulsory report (non-radar) or 7 minutes (radar), then follow the filed flight plan. Go to the destination aid, start descent at the last EAT (or ETA), fly the approach and land within 30 minutes. In VMC: stay VMC and land at the nearest suitable aerodrome.","hook":"76 = radio nix. 20 without radar, 7 with radar, land within 30.","steps":[{"label":"Squawk 7600","sub":"radio failure","tip":"In VMC simply continue VMC, land at the nearest suitable aerodrome and report arrival by the quickest means."},{"label":"Hold level & speed","sub":"20 min (no radar) / 7 min (radar)","tip":"Non-radar: 20 min after failing to report over a compulsory point. Radar: 7 min after the later of reaching the assigned level/MFA, squawking 7600, or missing a report."},{"label":"Filed flight plan","sub":"adjust level & speed","tip":"If being vectored or on an RNAV offset, rejoin the route no later than the next significant point."},{"label":"Destination aid","sub":"hold if needed","tip":"Proceed to the navigation aid or fix serving the destination."},{"label":"Descend at EAT","sub":"or ETA if no EAT","tip":"Start descent at, or as close as possible to, the last acknowledged EAT; if none, the flight-plan ETA."},{"label":"Approach & land","sub":"within 30 min","tip":"Fly the normal instrument approach and land, if possible, within 30 minutes after the ETA or last EAT, whichever is later."}]},{"id":"vmc","short":"VMC minima","part":"VFR, special VFR and IFR","type":"svg","fig":"reg-vmc","icon":"cloud","ask":"How much weather does VFR need?","dgca":true,"revise":true,"takeaway":"At and above 10,000 ft: 8 km visibility. Below that: 5 km. Everywhere stay 1,500 m horizontally and 1,000 ft vertically from cloud, except at or below 3,000 ft AMSL (or 1,000 ft above terrain) in Class F and G, where it is clear of cloud with the surface in sight.","hook":"8 up high, 5 down low; one-five-hundred metres and one thousand feet from the cloud."},{"id":"vfr","short":"VFR numbers","type":"numbers","icon":"eye","ask":"The VFR rules in numbers","dgca":true,"items":[{"k":"CTR: no VFR take-off/landing without clearance if ceiling below","v":"1,500 ft","note":"450 m"},{"k":"…or ground visibility below","v":"5 km"},{"k":"VFR hours (India)","v":"SR −20 to SS +20 min"},{"k":"VFR ceiling unless authorised","v":"FL 150"},{"k":"Never VFR above (RVSM)","v":"FL 290"},{"k":"Max seaward in controlled airspace","v":"100 NM"},{"k":"Over congested area","v":"1,000 ft above highest obstacle within 600 m"},{"k":"Elsewhere","v":"500 ft","note":"above ground or water"},{"k":"Semicircular levels apply above","v":"3,000 ft AGL"},{"k":"Reduced visibility allowed (ATS)","v":"3,000 m","note":"helicopters 1,500 m (India 1,000 m)"}]},{"id":"svfr","short":"Special VFR","takeaway":"Special VFR is a VFR flight cleared inside a control zone below VMC, with ground visibility of at least 1,500 m (1,000 m for PC 1/2 helicopters). In India only pilots with an instrument or instructor rating may fly it.","type":"pairs","icon":"fog","ask":"Special VFR: who, where, how much?","dgca":true,"pairs":[{"a":{"label":"What and where","body":"A VFR flight **cleared by ATC to operate in a control zone below VMC**. Approved by the **approach control** unit, one request at a time, when traffic permits."},"b":{"label":"Visibility","body":"Ground visibility **≥ 1,500 m**; Performance Class 1/2 helicopters **≥ 1,000 m** (state in field 18 and on RTF)."},"rule":"SVFR exists only inside a CTR."},{"a":{"label":"Who may fly it (India)","body":"Only pilots holding an **instrument rating, Assistant Flight Instructor or Flight Instructor rating**."},"b":{"label":"Purpose","body":"Enter the CTR to land, take off and leave, cross it, or fly locally within it."},"rule":""}]},{"id":"ifr","short":"IFR minimum levels","takeaway":"Without a published minimum, IFR flies 2,000 ft above the highest obstacle within 8 km over mountains, 1,000 ft elsewhere. Cancel IFR only for a stretch of uninterrupted VMC.","type":"pairs","icon":"mountain","ask":"IFR minimum levels and changing rules","dgca":true,"pairs":[{"a":{"label":"Over high terrain / mountains","body":"At least **600 m (2,000 ft)** above the highest obstacle within **8 km** of the estimated position."},"b":{"label":"Elsewhere","body":"At least **300 m (1,000 ft)** above the highest obstacle within **8 km**."},"rule":"Only where the State has not set a minimum flight altitude."},{"a":{"label":"IFR → VFR","body":"Tell ATS \"**IFR flight cancelled**\" and the changes; only if you expect a **reasonable period of uninterrupted VMC**."},"b":{"label":"VFR → IFR","body":"Send the flight-plan changes (or file) and **obtain a clearance** before going IFR in controlled airspace."},"rule":"Cancelling IFR is your choice; going IFR needs ATC."}]},{"id":"distress","short":"Distress or urgency","part":"Signals","type":"classify","icon":"alert","ask":"Distress or urgency?","dgca":true,"takeaway":"Distress (grave and imminent danger): SOS, MAYDAY, red rockets fired one at a time, red parachute flare. Urgency (must land, no immediate help): landing lights or nav lights switched on and off repeatedly. Very urgent message about safety: XXX or PAN PAN.","buckets":[{"id":"d","label":"Distress"},{"id":"u","label":"Urgency"}],"items":[{"text":"MAYDAY","bucket":"d"},{"text":"SOS in Morse","bucket":"d"},{"text":"Red rockets fired one at a time at short intervals","bucket":"d"},{"text":"Parachute flare showing red","bucket":"d"},{"text":"PAN PAN","bucket":"u"},{"text":"XXX by radiotelegraphy","bucket":"u"},{"text":"Landing lights switched on and off repeatedly","bucket":"u","why":"Means: I must land but need no immediate assistance."},{"text":"Navigation lights switched on and off (unlike normal flashing)","bucket":"u"}]},{"id":"intercept","short":"Interception signals","type":"stamp","icon":"shield","ask":"Interception: what does this signal mean?","dgca":true,"takeaway":"Rocking wings and flashing lights from above, ahead and to the left, then a slow turn: you have been intercepted, follow me. Abrupt climbing break-away: you may proceed. Gear down, steady landing lights over the runway: land here. Squawk 7700, call on 121.5 MHz, and follow visual signals over any other radio instruction.","hook":"Rock = follow, Break = go, Gear = land.","stamps":[{"id":"f","label":"Follow me"},{"id":"p","label":"You may proceed"},{"id":"l","label":"Land here"},{"id":"ina","label":"Aerodrome inadequate"},{"id":"cc","label":"Cannot comply"},{"id":"dis","label":"In distress"}],"cases":[{"text":"Interceptor rocks its wings and flashes nav lights from slightly above, ahead and to your left, then turns slowly.","answer":"f","why":"Series 1. Reply: rock wings, flash lights and follow."},{"text":"Interceptor makes an abrupt climbing turn of 90° or more away from you, not crossing your path.","answer":"p","why":"Series 2. Reply: rock wings."},{"text":"Interceptor lowers its gear, shows steady landing lights and overflies the runway.","answer":"l","why":"Series 3."},{"text":"You raise gear and flash landing lights over the runway at 1,000–2,000 ft, circling.","answer":"ina","why":"Series 4 (helicopters 170–330 ft)."},{"text":"You switch all lights on and off regularly.","answer":"cc","why":"Series 5."},{"text":"You flash all lights irregularly.","answer":"dis","why":"Series 6."}]},{"id":"intercept-do","short":"If intercepted","takeaway":"When intercepted: follow the interceptor, call on 121.5 MHz, squawk 7700, and if instructions conflict, obey the interceptor's visual signals or radio over anyone else's.","type":"bullets","icon":"radio","ask":"What the intercepted pilot does","tier":"exam","groups":[{"title":"At once","icon":"zap","points":["Follow the interceptor's instructions","Call on **121.5 MHz** (repeat on **243 MHz**)","Squawk **Mode A 7700**","ADS-B / ADS-C emergency mode"]},{"title":"Conflicting instructions","icon":"alert","points":["Other radio vs interceptor's **visual** signals → follow the **visual**","Other radio vs interceptor's **radio** → follow the **interceptor's radio**","Ask for clarification either way"]},{"title":"Phrases (said twice)","icon":"list","points":["Interceptor: CALL SIGN, FOLLOW, DESCEND, YOU LAND, PROCEED","You: CALL SIGN, WILCO, CAN NOT, REPEAT, AM LOST, MAYDAY, HIJACK, LAND (place), DESCEND"]},{"title":"Warning of a P/R/D area","icon":"flag","points":["Ground projectiles every **10 s** bursting **red and green**"]}]},{"id":"tower-lights","short":"Tower light signals","type":"matrix","icon":"tower","ask":"Light signals from the tower","revise":true,"dgca":true,"takeaway":"Steady green: land / take off. Steady red: keep circling / stop. Green flashes: come back to land / taxi. Red flashes: aerodrome unsafe / clear the landing area. White flashes: land here and go to the apron / back to the start. Red pyrotechnic: do not land for now.","hook":"Steady = do it now; flashing = get ready.","corner":"Signal","cols":["Aircraft in flight","Aircraft on the ground"],"rows":[{"label":"Steady green","cells":[{"v":"Cleared to land","key":true},{"v":"Cleared for take-off","key":true}]},{"label":"Steady red","cells":["Give way to other aircraft and continue circling","Stop"]},{"label":"Green flashes","cells":["Return for landing","Cleared to taxi"]},{"label":"Red flashes","cells":[{"v":"Aerodrome unsafe, do not land","key":true},"Taxi clear of landing area in use"]},{"label":"White flashes","cells":["Land at this aerodrome and proceed to apron","Return to starting point"]},{"label":"Red pyrotechnic","cells":[{"v":"Do not land for the time being","key":true},"—"]}]},{"id":"tower-stamp","short":"Read the light","takeaway":"Green flashes on the ground mean taxi; steady green means land or take off; red flashes in the air mean the aerodrome is unsafe; steady red on the ground means stop; white flashes on the ground mean go back.","type":"stamp","icon":"check","ask":"What does the tower want?","stamps":[{"id":"gs","label":"Steady green"},{"id":"rs","label":"Steady red"},{"id":"gf","label":"Green flashes"},{"id":"rf","label":"Red flashes"},{"id":"wf","label":"White flashes"}],"cases":[{"text":"You are on the ground and want to taxi to the holding point.","answer":"gf"},{"text":"You are on final and the tower clears you to land.","answer":"gs"},{"text":"You are in the circuit; the aerodrome is unsafe, go elsewhere.","answer":"rf"},{"text":"You are taxiing and must stop at once.","answer":"rs"},{"text":"On the ground, go back to where you started.","answer":"wf"}]},{"id":"ack","short":"Acknowledging","takeaway":"Acknowledge a light signal in flight by rocking the wings by day or flashing landing lights twice by night; on the ground by moving ailerons or rudder by day.","type":"pairs","icon":"check","ask":"How do you acknowledge a light signal?","tier":"exam","pairs":[{"a":{"label":"In flight","body":"Day: **rock the wings** (not expected on base or final). Night: **flash landing lights twice** (or nav lights)."},"b":{"label":"On the ground","body":"Day: **move ailerons or rudder**. Night: **flash landing lights twice** (or nav lights)."},"rule":"Night is the same in both: flash twice."}]},{"id":"ground","short":"Ground signals","type":"gallery","icon":"grid","ask":"Signals laid out in the signal area","dgca":true,"takeaway":"Red square with two yellow diagonals: landing prohibited. One diagonal: take special care. White dumbbell: use only runways and taxiways. Landing T: land along the shaft towards the cross-arm. Double white cross: gliders flying. Black C on yellow: ATS reporting office.","hook":"Two stripes = no; one stripe = slow.","items":[{"name":"Landing prohibited","fig":"reg-gs-noland","rows":[["Looks like","Horizontal **red square** with **two yellow diagonals**"],["Means","Landings prohibited; the ban is **liable to be prolonged**"]]},{"name":"Special precautions","fig":"reg-gs-caution","rows":[["Looks like","Red square with **one yellow diagonal**"],["Means","Bad state of the manoeuvring area or other reason: take **special precautions** approaching and landing"]]},{"name":"Runways and taxiways only","fig":"reg-gs-dumbbell","rows":[["Looks like","Horizontal **white dumbbell**"],["Means","Land, take off **and taxi** on runways and taxiways only"]]},{"name":"Runways only for take-off and landing","fig":"reg-gs-dumbbell-bars","rows":[["Looks like","White dumbbell with a **black bar across each disc**"],["Means","Land and take off on runways only; **other movements need not** stay on paved surfaces"]]},{"name":"Area unfit for movement","fig":"reg-gs-cross","rows":[["Looks like","**White or yellow cross** on a runway or taxiway"],["Means","That part is **closed / unfit** for aircraft"]]},{"name":"Landing T","fig":"reg-gs-tee","rows":[["Looks like","White or orange **T**"],["Means","Land and take off **parallel to the shaft, towards the cross-arm**"],["At night","Lit or outlined in white lights"]]},{"name":"Right-hand circuit","fig":"reg-gs-righthand","rows":[["Looks like","Right-hand **arrow** in a conspicuous colour"],["Means","Turns to the **right** before landing and after take-off"]]},{"name":"ATS reporting office","fig":"reg-gs-c","rows":[["Looks like","Black **C** on yellow, displayed vertically"],["Means","Location of the ATS reporting office"]]},{"name":"Gliders flying","fig":"reg-gs-glider","rows":[["Looks like","**Double white cross** displayed horizontally"],["Means","The aerodrome is used by gliders and glider flights are in progress"]]},{"name":"Take-off direction","fig":"reg-gs-digits","rows":[["Looks like","Two digits displayed **vertically** at or near the tower"],["Means","Take-off direction in **tens of degrees magnetic** (e.g. 27 = 270°)"]]}]},{"id":"marshal","short":"Marshalling","takeaway":"Learn the core marshalling signals: straight ahead, turn, normal and emergency stop, brakes, chocks, start and cut engines, slow down, hold, all clear.","type":"tricks","icon":"user","ask":"Marshalling signals","dgca":true,"cue":"What the signalman does","cards":[{"name":"Straight ahead","trick":"Bent arms, wands moved up and down from chest to head height.","emoji":"⬆️"},{"name":"Normal stop","trick":"Arms out at 90°, slowly raised until the wands cross overhead.","emoji":"✋"},{"name":"Emergency stop","trick":"Arms and wands abruptly thrown to the top of the head, crossed.","emoji":"🛑"},{"name":"Set brakes","trick":"Open palm just above shoulder, closed into a fist; wait for thumbs-up.","emoji":"✊"},{"name":"Release brakes","trick":"Fist just above shoulder, opened into a palm.","emoji":"🖐️"},{"name":"Chocks inserted","trick":"Arms overhead, wands jabbed inward until they touch.","emoji":"🔒"},{"name":"Start engine","trick":"Right hand circles at head level; left arm points at the engine.","emoji":"🔄"},{"name":"Cut engines","trick":"Slicing motion across the throat.","emoji":"🔪"},{"name":"Slow down","trick":"Patting motion downwards, waist to knees.","emoji":"🐢"},{"name":"Move back","trick":"Arms rotate forward in front of the body at waist height.","emoji":"⏪"},{"name":"Hold position / stand by","trick":"Arms down at 45° to the sides.","emoji":"⏸️"},{"name":"All clear / affirmative","trick":"Right arm up with wand pointing up, or thumbs up.","emoji":"👍"},{"name":"Connect ground power","trick":"Hands form a \"T\" above the head.","emoji":"🔌"},{"name":"Dispatch aircraft","trick":"A standard salute, keeping eye contact until it taxis.","emoji":"🫡"},{"name":"Helicopter: land","trick":"Arms crossed with wands pointing down in front of the body.","emoji":"🚁"}]},{"id":"marshal-rules","short":"Signalman rules","takeaway":"Signalmen are DGCA-approved, wear fluorescent vests, use wands, bats or gloves (lit wands at night), and stand on the aircraft's left. Engines are numbered from the signalman's right.","type":"bullets","icon":"info","ask":"Rules for the signalman","tier":"exam","groups":[{"title":"Who","icon":"user","points":["Trained, qualified and **approved by the DGCA**","Wears a **fluorescent vest**"]},{"title":"Kit","icon":"zap","points":["Day: fluorescent wands, table-tennis bats or gloves","Night / low visibility: **illuminated wands**"]},{"title":"Where","icon":"target","points":["Fixed-wing: facing the aircraft, on its **left** side","Engines numbered from the signalman's **right to left** (No. 1 = port outer)"]},{"title":"Pilot to signalman","icon":"plane","points":["Brakes on: fingers extended, then clench fist","Insert chocks: palms out, hands cross in front of the face","Remove chocks: crossed hands move outward"]},{"title":"ARFF to crew","icon":"alert","points":["From the **left front**","Recommend evacuation: beckoning arm","Stop: arms crossed at the wrists in front of the head","Emergency contained: umpire's \"safe\" signal","Fire: fanning wand + point"]}]},{"id":"adiz","short":"Air defence clearance","type":"numbers","icon":"shield","ask":"Air defence clearance (ADC) numbers","tier":"exam","items":[{"k":"ADIZs in India","v":"N, S, E, W, Central","note":"+ sub-ADIZ South East"},{"k":"Exempt local flights","v":"5 NM & 1,000 ft AGL","note":"around the ARP"},{"k":"Validity, scheduled","v":"EOBT −15 / +45 min"},{"k":"Validity, non-scheduled domestic","v":"EOBT −15 min / +3 h"},{"k":"International inbound: ADC from FIC","v":"10 min before","note":"entering Indian airspace"},{"k":"Same-day return ADC void if delayed","v":"> 30 min"}]},{"id":"levels","short":"Cruising levels","part":"Cruising levels","type":"svg","fig":"reg-semicircular","icon":"compass","ask":"Which level for my track?","dgca":true,"takeaway":"Magnetic track 000–179°: IFR at odd thousands (FL 50, 70 … 290, 310 … 410), VFR at odd + 500 (FL 55, 75). Track 180–359°: IFR even (FL 60, 80 … 280, 300 … 400), VFR even + 500. RVSM (1,000 ft) applies FL 290–410; above FL 410 levels go in 4,000 ft steps (east 450, 490; west 430, 470). No VFR in or above RVSM airspace.","hook":"East is odd, west is even (Odd East, Even West: OE-EW)."},{"id":"levels-stamp","short":"Pick the level","takeaway":"Eastbound (000–179°) IFR flies odd levels and VFR odd + 500; westbound (180–359°) IFR flies even levels and VFR even + 500.","type":"stamp","icon":"check","ask":"Pick the correct cruising level","stamps":[{"id":"a","label":"FL 70"},{"id":"b","label":"FL 80"},{"id":"c","label":"FL 75"},{"id":"d","label":"FL 85"},{"id":"e","label":"FL 330"},{"id":"f","label":"FL 340"}],"cases":[{"text":"IFR, magnetic track 090°, around 7,000–8,000 ft.","answer":"a"},{"text":"IFR, magnetic track 270°, around 7,000–8,000 ft.","answer":"b"},{"text":"VFR, magnetic track 045°, around 7,000–8,000 ft.","answer":"c"},{"text":"VFR, magnetic track 200°, around 8,000–9,000 ft.","answer":"d"},{"text":"IFR in RVSM airspace, magnetic track 120°, around FL 330–340.","answer":"e"},{"text":"IFR in RVSM airspace, magnetic track 300°, around FL 330–340.","answer":"f"}]},{"id":"unlawful","short":"Hijack levels","takeaway":"A hijacked aircraft keeps its track and level if it can; if it must deviate without contact, it flies 500 ft off the normal IFR levels where 1,000 ft separation applies.","type":"pairs","icon":"lock","ask":"Hijacked: what level do you fly?","tier":"exam","pairs":[{"a":{"label":"If you can","body":"Keep the **assigned track and level** until you can tell ATS or are in radar/ADS-B cover. Broadcast warnings on the VHF in use or 121.5; use the transponder and data link."},"b":{"label":"If you must leave it","body":"Follow Doc 7030 contingency procedures, or fly a level offset from normal IFR levels by **500 ft** (where 1,000 ft separation applies) or **1,000 ft** (where 2,000 ft applies)."},"rule":"Offset = half the vertical separation minimum."}]},{"id":"numbers","short":"Numbers to know","part":"Numbers","type":"numbers","icon":"hash","ask":"Numbers to know","items":[{"k":"Overtaking zone","v":"< 70°","note":"from the other's tail"},{"k":"Red / green light arc","v":"110°"},{"k":"White tail light arc","v":"140°"},{"k":"Aerobatics complete by","v":"2,000 ft AGL"},{"k":"No aerobatics within","v":"2 NM below 6,000 ft"},{"k":"Formation (ICAO)","v":"0.5 NM / 100 ft"},{"k":"File flight plan","v":"≥ 3 h, ≤ 120 h","note":"before EOBT"},{"k":"Delay to report","v":"15 min"},{"k":"Time estimate error to report","v":"> 2 min"},{"k":"Mach / TAS error to report","v":"M 0.02 / 10 kt"},{"k":"RCF non-radar","v":"20 min"},{"k":"RCF radar","v":"7 min"},{"k":"RCF land within","v":"30 min"},{"k":"VMC at/above 10,000 ft","v":"8 km"},{"k":"VMC below 10,000 ft","v":"5 km"},{"k":"Distance from cloud","v":"1,500 m / 1,000 ft"},{"k":"Special VFR","v":"1,500 m","note":"PC1/2 helicopters 1,000 m"},{"k":"IFR min level, mountains","v":"2,000 ft in 8 km"},{"k":"IFR min level, elsewhere","v":"1,000 ft in 8 km"},{"k":"ATS clocks","v":"± 30 s","note":"data link ± 1 s"},{"k":"RVSM band","v":"FL 290–410"}]},{"id":"quiz","short":"Quick check","type":"quiz","icon":"check","ask":"Quick check","questions":[{"q":"Two aeroplanes converge at the same level. Which gives way?","options":["The faster one","The one that has the other on its right","The one that has the other on its left","The higher one"],"answer":1},{"q":"Where no minimum IFR altitude is set, over mountains you must be at least:","options":["1,000 ft above obstacles within 8 km","2,000 ft above obstacles within 8 km","2,000 ft above obstacles within 5 NM","1,500 ft above obstacles within 8 km"],"answer":1},{"q":"A series of red flashes to an aircraft in flight means:","options":["Give way and continue circling","Aerodrome unsafe, do not land","Do not land for the time being","Return for landing"],"answer":1},{"q":"In non-radar airspace after radio failure in IMC, you keep the last assigned level and speed for:","options":["7 minutes","10 minutes","20 minutes","30 minutes"],"answer":2},{"q":"The white tail navigation light covers an arc of:","options":["110°","140°","70°","180°"],"answer":1},{"q":"Special VFR in a control zone needs a ground visibility of at least:","options":["800 m","1,000 m","1,500 m","5 km"],"answer":2},{"q":"IFR, magnetic track 185°, non-RVSM levels below FL 290: which level?","options":["FL 70","FL 80","FL 75","FL 85"],"answer":1},{"q":"An interceptor lowers its gear and overflies the runway with steady landing lights. It means:","options":["You may proceed","Follow me","Land at this aerodrome","Aerodrome inadequate"],"answer":2}]}],"id":"reg-04","notesHtml":"<h3 id=\"n-applicability-and-responsibility-p-77-78\">Applicability and responsibility (p.77–78)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Rules apply to all aircraft <strong>over Indian territory</strong> and to <strong>Indian-registered aircraft wherever they are</strong>, unless they conflict with the rules of the State overflown. Over the high seas where a State provides ATS by regional agreement, the \"appropriate ATS authority\" is that State's.</li>\n<li>Every operation in flight or on the movement area follows the <strong>general rules</strong>, plus in flight either <strong>VFR or IFR</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>PIC</strong> is responsible for complying with the rules <strong>whether manipulating the controls or not</strong>, and may depart from them only when <strong>absolutely necessary for safety</strong>. The PIC has <strong>final authority</strong> as to the disposition of the aircraft.</li>\n<li><strong>Pre-flight action</strong>: become familiar with all available information; for flights away from the vicinity of an aerodrome and <strong>all IFR flights</strong>, study current weather reports and forecasts, fuel requirements and an alternative course of action.</li>\n<li><strong>Safety-sensitive personnel</strong> must not work under the influence of any psychoactive substance that impairs performance, nor engage in problematic use.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-general-rules-p-78-81\">General rules (p.78–81)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>No <strong>negligent or reckless</strong> operation.</li>\n<li><strong>Minimum safe height</strong> over congested areas or open-air assemblies: high enough to land <strong>without undue hazard</strong> after an emergency (unless taking off/landing or with DG permission). DG may permit crop spraying, mining exploration at specified heights.</li>\n<li><strong>Cruising levels</strong>: <strong>flight levels</strong> at or above the lowest usable FL / above TA; <strong>altitudes</strong> below the lowest usable FL / at or below TA.</li>\n<li><strong>Dropping or spraying</strong>: only under <strong>Rule 26</strong> of the Aircraft Rules 1937 and as cleared by ATS. <strong>Parachute descents</strong>: only in emergency or by written order of the Central Government.</li>\n<li><strong>Towing</strong>: no aircraft shall tow an aircraft; <strong>towing of aircraft is not permitted in India</strong>; objects only as prescribed by the DG.</li>\n<li><strong>Aerobatics</strong>: licensed pilot (or trainee with an instructor); passengers' <strong>written consent</strong>; everyone strapped in; <strong>complete the manoeuvre by 600 m (2,000 ft) AGL</strong> (or higher if the C of A says). Not hazardous to traffic; <strong>not within 2 NM of an aerodrome perimeter below 1,800 m (6,000 ft)</strong>; not over a city, town, village or populous area, nor over a public gathering unless the promoters applied in writing to the DG.</li>\n<li><strong>Formation flight</strong>: <strong>not allowed for civil aircraft in India</strong>. ICAO allows it by pre-arrangement: formation acts as a <strong>single aircraft</strong> for navigation and reporting, separation within it is the leader's and pilots' responsibility, each aircraft within <strong>1 km (0.5 NM)</strong> laterally/longitudinally and <strong>30 m (100 ft)</strong> vertically of the leader.</li>\n<li><strong>ACAS RA</strong>: nothing relieves the PIC of taking avoiding action, including following resolution advisories.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-right-of-way-p-80-81\">Right of way (p.80–81)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The aircraft with right of way <strong>maintains heading and speed</strong>. The one giving way must not pass <strong>over, under or in front</strong> unless well clear, allowing for wake turbulence.</li>\n<li><strong>Head-on</strong>: <strong>both alter heading to the right</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Converging</strong> at about the same level: the aircraft that has the other <strong>on its right gives way</strong>, except: <strong>power-driven heavier-than-air gives way to airships, gliders and balloons; airships to gliders and balloons; gliders to balloons</strong>; power-driven aircraft give way to aircraft <strong>seen to be towing</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Overtaking</strong>: approaching from the rear within <strong>70°</strong> of the other's plane of symmetry (at night you could see neither navigation light, only the white tail light). The overtaken aircraft has right of way; the overtaker keeps clear by <strong>altering heading to the right</strong>, whether climbing, descending or level, until entirely past and clear.</li>\n<li><strong>Landing</strong>: aircraft in flight or on the ground/water give way to aircraft <strong>landing or on final</strong>. Of two landing aircraft, the <strong>higher gives way to the lower</strong>, but the lower must not cut in front of one on final; <strong>power-driven heavier-than-air aircraft give way to gliders</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Emergency landing</strong>: give way to an aircraft known to be compelled to land.</li>\n<li><strong>Take-off</strong>: aircraft taxiing on the manoeuvring area give way to aircraft <strong>taking off or about to take off</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Surface movement</strong>: head-on → <strong>stop or turn right</strong>; converging → the one with the other <strong>on its right</strong> gives way; overtaken has right of way. Stop at <strong>all runway-holding positions</strong> unless cleared, and at <strong>all lighted stop bars</strong> (proceed when they are switched off).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-lights-to-be-displayed-p-81-82-113-115\">Lights to be displayed (p.81–82, 113–115)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sunset to sunrise</strong>, aircraft <strong>in flight</strong>: <strong>anti-collision lights</strong> and <strong>navigation lights</strong>; no lights that can be mistaken for them. Landing lights and floodlights may be added for conspicuity.</li>\n<li>On the <strong>movement area</strong> at night: navigation lights; lights showing the <strong>extremities</strong> of the structure (unless stationary and lit); lights to attract attention; lights showing <strong>engines running</strong>.</li>\n<li>Aircraft fitted with anti-collision lights display them <strong>also by day</strong>, in flight and on the movement area. The pilot may switch off or dim flashing lights that hinder duties or dazzle observers.</li>\n<li><strong>Navigation lights</strong>: <strong>red</strong> on the left, <strong>110°</strong> (from dead ahead to 110° left); <strong>green</strong> on the right, <strong>110°</strong>; <strong>white</strong> tail light, <strong>140°</strong> (70° each side of dead astern).</li>\n<li>Seeing <strong>red + green</strong> → head-on; only <strong>white</strong> → it is moving away; <strong>red + white</strong> → crossing right to left; <strong>green + white</strong> → crossing left to right. A light that stays at a <strong>constant relative bearing</strong> = collision risk: <strong>green</strong> → you have right of way; <strong>red</strong> → give way by turning right. (\"Red to red, green to green: safe.\")</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-simulated-instrument-flight-p-82\">Simulated instrument flight (p.82)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Only with <strong>fully functioning dual controls</strong> and a qualified <strong>safety pilot</strong> in a control seat with adequate forward and side vision (or a competent observer in communication with the safety pilot).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-at-or-near-an-aerodrome-p-82\">At or near an aerodrome (p.82)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Observe other traffic; conform with or avoid the pattern; <strong>all turns to the left</strong> after take-off and before landing unless told otherwise; land and take off <strong>into wind</strong> unless safety or traffic says otherwise.</li>\n<li>Where a tower operates: keep a <strong>continuous listening watch</strong> on its frequency (or watch for visual signals) and get <strong>prior authorisation</strong> (radio or visual) for taxi, take-off and landing manoeuvres.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-flight-plans-p-83-84\">Flight plans (p.83–84)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>In India, every flight's information goes to the ATS unit, except <strong>nano RPA up to 50 ft</strong> and <strong>micro RPA up to 200 ft AGL</strong> in uncontrolled airspace or enclosed premises.</li>\n<li>A flight plan is required for: any flight to receive <strong>ATC service</strong>; any <strong>IFR flight in advisory airspace</strong>; flights in designated areas/routes to facilitate FIS, alerting and SAR, or coordination with military/adjacent units to avoid interception; and <strong>any flight across international borders</strong>.</li>\n<li>Before departure the operator ensures approval for any <strong>RNP/RVSM/RCP/RSP</strong> specification on the route.</li>\n<li>Submit <strong>at least 180 minutes (3 h)</strong> before and <strong>not earlier than 120 hours</strong> before <strong>EOBT</strong>. <strong>RPA</strong>: at least <strong>24 hours</strong> before ETD.</li>\n<li>Delay of <strong>15 minutes or more</strong>: notify ATC of the revised time (amend, or cancel and refile).</li>\n<li>Contents: relevant items <strong>up to and including the alternate aerodrome(s)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Changes</strong>: IFR and controlled VFR flights report <strong>all changes</strong>; other VFR report <strong>significant</strong> changes. Wrong <strong>endurance or persons on board</strong> at departure is a significant change.</li>\n<li><strong>Closing</strong>: an <strong>arrival report</strong> in person, by RTF or data link, at the earliest moment after landing, for any flight whose plan covered the whole flight or the rest of it to destination.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-time-p-84\">Time (p.84)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>UTC</strong>, hours and minutes (seconds if needed) of the 24-hour day from midnight. <strong>Time check before a controlled flight</strong>. ATS clocks within <strong>±30 s</strong> of UTC; data link time within <strong>1 s</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-atc-clearances-and-deviations-p-85-86\">ATC clearances and deviations (p.85–86)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Clearance must be obtained <strong>before</strong> a controlled flight, requested by submitting a flight plan. Adhere to the <strong>current flight plan</strong> unless a change is requested and cleared.</li>\n<li><strong>Off track</strong>: adjust heading to regain track <strong>forthwith</strong>. <strong>Deviation from assigned Mach/IAS</strong>: inform ATS <strong>immediately</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mach/TAS</strong> at cruising level varies by <strong>±M 0.02</strong> or <strong>±10 kt (19 km/h) TAS</strong> from the flight plan: inform ATS.</li>\n<li><strong>Time estimate</strong> for the next reporting point, FIR boundary or destination changes by <strong>more than 2 minutes</strong>: notify ATS (automatic via ADS-C where active).</li>\n<li><strong>VFR controlled flight losing VMC</strong>: request an amended clearance to stay VMC (to destination/alternate or to leave controlled airspace); or if none, continue VMC and tell ATC you are leaving or landing at the nearest suitable aerodrome; or in a <strong>CTR</strong> request <strong>special VFR</strong>; or request <strong>IFR</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Position reports</strong>: time and level over each <strong>compulsory reporting point</strong>, and others on request.</li>\n<li><strong>Communications</strong>: controlled flights keep a <strong>continuous air-ground voice watch</strong>; <strong>SELCAL</strong> satisfies this; the watch continues <strong>after CPDLC</strong> is established.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-radio-communication-failure-p-86-87\">Radio communication failure (p.86–87)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>In VMC</strong>: continue in VMC, <strong>land at the nearest suitable aerodrome</strong>, report arrival to ATC by the quickest means (or complete IFR if advisable).</li>\n<li><strong>In IMC / IFR</strong>: <strong>non-radar</strong> airspace: keep last assigned <strong>speed and level</strong> (or minimum flight altitude if higher) for <strong>20 minutes</strong> after failing to report over a compulsory reporting point, then follow the <strong>filed flight plan</strong>. <strong>Radar</strong> airspace: the same for <strong>7 minutes</strong> after the later of reaching the last assigned level/MFA, <strong>setting 7600</strong>, or missing a compulsory report.</li>\n<li>If being <strong>vectored</strong> or offset by RNAV, rejoin the flight-plan route <strong>no later than the next significant point</strong>.</li>\n<li>Proceed to the destination aid/fix, hold if needed, <strong>begin descent at or close to the EAT</strong> last acknowledged (or the flight-plan <strong>ETA</strong> if no EAT), fly the normal instrument approach, and <strong>land within 30 minutes</strong> after ETA or last EAT, whichever is later.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-vmc-minima-p-87-88\">VMC minima (p.87–88)</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Altitude band</th><th>Class</th><th>Flight visibility</th><th>Distance from cloud</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>At and above 3,050 m (10,000 ft) AMSL</td><td>A–G</td><td><strong>8 km</strong></td><td><strong>1,500 m horizontally, 300 m (1,000 ft) vertically</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Below 10,000 ft and above 3,000 ft AMSL or 1,000 ft above terrain (higher)</td><td>A–G</td><td><strong>5 km</strong></td><td>1,500 m horizontally, 1,000 ft vertically</td></tr><tr><td>At or below 3,000 ft AMSL or 1,000 ft above terrain (higher)</td><td>A–E</td><td><strong>5 km</strong></td><td>1,500 m horizontally, 1,000 ft vertically</td></tr><tr><td>same band</td><td>F, G</td><td><strong>5 km</strong></td><td><strong>clear of cloud and in sight of the surface</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li>If the TA is below 10,000 ft, use <strong>FL 100</strong> instead of 10,000 ft. Class A minima are guidance only (VFR not accepted in Class A).</li>\n<li>ATS may permit visibility down to <strong>3,000 m</strong> (slow enough to see and avoid, or low traffic, aerial work); helicopters down to <strong>1,500 m</strong>. In India, F and G low band: <strong>3,000 m</strong>, helicopters <strong>1,000 m</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-visual-flight-rules-p-88-89\">Visual flight rules (p.88–89)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Without ATC clearance, VFR must not take off, land or enter the ATZ/circuit in a <strong>CTR</strong> when <strong>ceiling &lt; 450 m (1,500 ft)</strong> or <strong>ground visibility &lt; 5 km</strong>.</li>\n<li>India: VFR only from <strong>20 minutes before sunrise to 20 minutes after sunset</strong> (except local and flying-club training flights cleared by ATC). The book defines <strong>flight by night</strong> as from <strong>½ hour after sunset to ½ hour before sunrise</strong>.</li>\n<li>Unless authorised, VFR not <strong>above FL 150</strong>, not at <strong>transonic/supersonic</strong> speed, not <strong>more than 100 NM seaward</strong> in controlled airspace. Never above <strong>FL 290</strong> where RVSM applies.</li>\n<li>VFR minimum heights: over congested areas or assemblies <strong>300 m (1,000 ft) above the highest obstacle within 600 m</strong>; elsewhere <strong>150 m (500 ft)</strong> above ground or water.</li>\n<li>VFR in level cruise <strong>above 900 m (3,000 ft)</strong> above ground/water uses the <strong>cruising levels for its track</strong>.</li>\n<li>VFR complies with ATC in <strong>Classes B, C, D</strong>, as aerodrome traffic at controlled aerodromes, and as special VFR.</li>\n<li>VFR changing to IFR: send changes to the flight plan, or file one, and <strong>get a clearance</strong> before proceeding IFR in controlled airspace.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-special-vfr-p-89\">Special VFR (p.89)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Authorised by the <strong>approach control unit</strong> when traffic permits, each request individually.</li>\n<li><strong>Ground visibility ≥ 1,500 m</strong>: may enter a CTR to land, take off and depart, cross, or operate locally. <strong>Performance Class 1 and 2 helicopters: ≥ 1,000 m</strong> (state it in field 18 and on RTF).</li>\n<li>In India, special VFR only by pilots with an <strong>instrument rating, Assistant Flight Instructor or Flight Instructor rating</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-instrument-flight-rules-p-89-91\">Instrument flight rules (p.89–91)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Suitable instruments and navigation equipment for the route.</li>\n<li><strong>Minimum level</strong> (where the State has none): over <strong>high terrain or mountains</strong>, <strong>600 m (2,000 ft)</strong> above the highest obstacle within <strong>8 km</strong> of the estimated position; elsewhere <strong>300 m (1,000 ft)</strong> within <strong>8 km</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>IFR to VFR</strong>: tell ATS specifically \"<strong>IFR flight cancelled</strong>\"; do so only if the flight will continue for a reasonable time in <strong>uninterrupted VMC</strong>.</li>\n<li>IFR in controlled airspace: cruising levels (or cruise climb) from the tables; modified tables above <strong>FL 410</strong>. Outside controlled airspace: levels appropriate to track (except at or below <strong>3,000 ft AMSL</strong> if specified); keep a watch and report position where required. Advisory service users: flight plan changes are <strong>not</strong> subject to clearance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-distress-and-urgency-signals-p-91-92\">Distress and urgency signals (p.91–92)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Distress</strong> (grave and imminent danger, immediate help): <strong>SOS</strong> (···---···), <strong>MAYDAY</strong>, data-link MAYDAY, <strong>red rockets or shells fired one at a time at short intervals</strong>, <strong>red parachute flare</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Urgency</strong> (must land, no immediate help needed): <strong>repeated switching on and off of landing lights</strong>, or of navigation lights (distinct from flashing).</li>\n<li><strong>Very urgent message</strong> about the safety of a ship, aircraft, vehicle or person: <strong>XXX</strong>, <strong>PAN PAN</strong>, data-link PAN PAN.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-adiz-and-air-defence-clearance-p-92\">ADIZ and air defence clearance (p.92)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>ADIZs: <strong>North, South, East, West, Central</strong> and sub-ADIZ South East.</li>\n<li><strong>ADC</strong> required before take-off through ATC for flights to, through or within an ADIZ, except <strong>local flights within 5 NM of the ARP and below 1,000 ft AGL</strong>.</li>\n<li>One ADC is valid for the <strong>entire route</strong>. Flights entering from abroad get ADC from the FIC <strong>10 minutes before entering Indian airspace</strong>.</li>\n<li>Validity: <strong>EOBT −15 to +45 minutes</strong> (scheduled, RPL/FPL); non-scheduled domestic <strong>−15 minutes to +3 hours</strong>. If communications fail at departure, a radio-equipped aircraft may take off and get ADC immediately after airborne.</li>\n<li>Flying clubs without ATC: ADC from the nearest <strong>IAF ATC unit</strong>. Return flights the same day may get ADC for the return too, but need a fresh one if <strong>delayed more than 30 minutes</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-interception-p-92-96\">Interception (p.92–96)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intercepted aircraft</strong>: follow the interceptor's instructions; contact on <strong>121.5 MHz</strong> (repeat on <strong>243 MHz</strong> if practicable); <strong>squawk A 7700</strong>; select ADS-B/ADS-C emergency mode. If radio instructions from other sources conflict with the interceptor's <strong>visual</strong> signals, <strong>follow the visual signals</strong> and ask for clarification; if they conflict with the interceptor's <strong>radio</strong> instructions, follow the <strong>interceptor's radio</strong> instructions.</li>\n<li>Call signs: \"INTERCEPT CONTROL\", \"INTERCEPTOR (call sign)\", \"INTERCEPTED AIRCRAFT\". Phrases are said twice: interceptor — CALL SIGN, FOLLOW, DESCEND, YOU LAND, PROCEED; intercepted — CALL SIGN, WILCO, CAN NOT, REPEAT, AM LOST, MAYDAY, HIJACK, LAND (place), DESCEND.</li>\n<li><strong>Series 1</strong>: interceptor <strong>rocks wings and flashes nav lights</strong> from slightly above, ahead and normally to the <strong>left</strong> (right for a helicopter), then a slow level turn = \"<strong>You have been intercepted, follow me</strong>\". Reply: rock wings, flash lights, follow.</li>\n<li><strong>Series 2</strong>: <strong>abrupt break-away</strong>, climbing turn of 90° or more without crossing the intercepted aircraft's path = \"<strong>You may proceed</strong>\". Reply: rock wings.</li>\n<li><strong>Series 3</strong>: <strong>gear down, steady landing lights, overfly the runway</strong> = \"<strong>Land at this aerodrome</strong>\". Reply: same and land if safe.</li>\n<li><strong>Series 4</strong> (from intercepted): <strong>gear up and flashing landing lights</strong> while passing over the runway at <strong>1,000–2,000 ft</strong> (helicopter <strong>170–330 ft</strong>), circling = \"<strong>Aerodrome is inadequate</strong>\".</li>\n<li><strong>Series 5</strong>: <strong>regular switching on and off of all lights</strong> = \"<strong>Cannot comply</strong>\". <strong>Series 6</strong>: <strong>irregular flashing of all lights</strong> = \"<strong>In distress</strong>\".</li>\n<li>Warning of a <strong>restricted, prohibited or danger area</strong>: projectiles from the ground every <strong>10 seconds</strong> bursting into <strong>red and green</strong> lights/stars.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-light-signals-from-the-tower-p-96-97\">Light signals from the tower (p.96–97)</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>In flight</th><th>On the ground</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Steady green</td><td><strong>Cleared to land</strong></td><td><strong>Cleared for take-off</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Steady red</td><td><strong>Give way and continue circling</strong></td><td><strong>Stop</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Green flashes</td><td><strong>Return for landing</strong></td><td><strong>Cleared to taxi</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Red flashes</td><td><strong>Aerodrome unsafe, do not land</strong></td><td><strong>Taxi clear of the landing area</strong></td></tr><tr><td>White flashes</td><td><strong>Land here and proceed to apron</strong></td><td><strong>Return to starting point</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Red pyrotechnic</td><td><strong>Do not land for the time being</strong> (whatever earlier instructions)</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li>Acknowledgement in flight: by day <strong>rock the wings</strong> (not expected on base or final); by night <strong>flash landing lights twice</strong> (or nav lights). On the ground: by day <strong>move ailerons or rudder</strong>; by night flash landing (or nav) lights twice.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-visual-ground-signals-p-97-98\">Visual ground signals (p.97–98)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Red square with two yellow diagonals</strong>: <strong>landing prohibited</strong>, likely to be prolonged.</li>\n<li><strong>Red square with one yellow diagonal</strong>: <strong>special precautions</strong> in approach/landing (bad manoeuvring area).</li>\n<li><strong>White dumbbell</strong>: land, take off and <strong>taxi on runways and taxiways only</strong>. <strong>Dumbbell with black bars across each disc</strong>: land and take off on runways only; other manoeuvres need not be confined.</li>\n<li><strong>White or yellow crosses</strong> on runways/taxiways: area <strong>unfit for movement</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>White or orange landing T</strong>: land and take off <strong>parallel to the shaft towards the cross-arm</strong> (lit or outlined in white at night).</li>\n<li><strong>Two digits displayed vertically</strong> near the tower: take-off direction in tens of degrees magnetic.</li>\n<li><strong>Right-hand arrow</strong> of conspicuous colour: <strong>right-hand circuit</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Black C on yellow</strong>, displayed vertically: <strong>ATS reporting office</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Double white cross</strong>: <strong>gliders operating</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-marshalling-p-98-112\">Marshalling (p.98–112)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Only signalmen <strong>trained, qualified and approved by the DGCA</strong> may guide aircraft; they wear a <strong>fluorescent vest</strong>; by day fluorescent wands, bats or gloves; <strong>illuminated wands at night</strong> or in low visibility.</li>\n<li>Signalman faces the aircraft, for fixed-wing on the <strong>left side</strong> where the pilot sees best. Engines are numbered from the <strong>signalman's right to left</strong> (No. 1 = port outer).</li>\n<li>Key signals: <strong>straight ahead</strong> (bent arms, wands up and down chest to head); <strong>turn</strong> (one arm out at 90°, the other beckoning); <strong>normal stop</strong> (arms out at 90°, slowly raised until wands cross); <strong>emergency stop</strong> (abruptly raise and cross); <strong>set brakes</strong> (open palm closed to fist); <strong>release brakes</strong> (fist opened); <strong>chocks inserted</strong> (wands jab inward to touch) / <strong>removed</strong> (jab outward); <strong>start engine</strong> (right hand circling, left pointing at engine); <strong>cut engines</strong> (slicing motion across the throat); <strong>slow down</strong> (patting downwards); <strong>move back</strong> (arms rotate forward at waist); <strong>all clear</strong> (right arm up / thumbs up); <strong>hold position</strong> (arms down at 45°); <strong>dispatch</strong> (salute); <strong>do not touch controls</strong>; <strong>connect/disconnect ground power</strong> (a \"T\" over the head).</li>\n<li>Helicopters: <strong>hover</strong> (arms out at 90°), <strong>move up/down</strong> (palms up/down), <strong>move left/right</strong>, <strong>land</strong> (arms crossed downwards in front).</li>\n<li>ARFF emergency hand signals (from the <strong>left front</strong>): <strong>recommend evacuation</strong> (beckoning arm), <strong>recommended stop</strong> (arms crossed at wrists in front of head), <strong>emergency contained</strong> (umpire's \"safe\" signal), <strong>fire</strong> (fanning wand pointing at the fire).</li>\n<li>Pilot to signalman: <strong>brakes engaged</strong> (fingers extended, then clench fist); <strong>released</strong> (fist, then extend fingers); <strong>insert chocks</strong> (palms out, hands move in to cross in front of face); <strong>remove chocks</strong> (crossed hands move out).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-cruising-levels-p-112-113\">Cruising levels (p.112–113)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>RVSM</strong> (1,000 ft separation) between <strong>FL 290 and FL 410 inclusive</strong> in India.</li>\n<li>Magnetic track <strong>000–179°</strong>: IFR <strong>odd</strong> levels (FL 10, 30, 50 … 290, 310 … 410), VFR <strong>odd + 500</strong> (FL 35, 55 … 285). Track <strong>180–359°</strong>: IFR <strong>even</strong> (FL 20, 40 … 280, 300 … 400), VFR <strong>even + 500</strong> (FL 45, 65 … 285).</li>\n<li>Above <strong>FL 410</strong>: 000–179 → FL 450, 490 …; 180–359 → FL 430, 470 … (2,000 ft steps each way).</li>\n<li>Non-RVSM (CVSM) above FL 290: eastbound 290, 330, 370, 410 …; westbound 310, 350, 390, 430 … (4,000 ft between same-direction levels).</li>\n<li><strong>VFR not allowed in and above RVSM airspace</strong>.</li>\n<li>Polar areas above 70° latitude use grid tracks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-unlawful-interference-p-115-116\">Unlawful interference (p.115–116)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Try to keep the <strong>assigned track and level</strong> until you can notify ATS or are in radar/ADS-B cover.</li>\n<li>If forced to leave track or level without RTF contact: broadcast warnings on the VHF in use or the emergency frequency, use transponder/data link, follow Doc 7030 contingency procedures, or else fly a level offset from normal IFR levels by <strong>500 ft where 1,000 ft separation applies</strong> (<strong>1,000 ft where 2,000 ft applies</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"n-exam-traps\">Exam traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Head-on: <strong>both turn right</strong>. Converging: the one with the other <strong>on its right</strong> gives way. Overtaking: overtaker turns <strong>right</strong>; within <strong>70°</strong> of the tail.</li>\n<li>Powered aircraft give way to <strong>airships, gliders, balloons</strong>; gliders give way only to <strong>balloons</strong>.</li>\n<li>Of two landing aircraft, the <strong>lower</strong> has priority, but powered aircraft still give way to <strong>gliders</strong>.</li>\n<li>Flight plan <strong>≥ 3 h before</strong>, <strong>≤ 120 h before</strong> EOBT; delay <strong>15 min</strong> → tell ATC.</li>\n<li>RCF: <strong>20 min</strong> (non-radar) / <strong>7 min</strong> (radar); land within <strong>30 min</strong> of ETA/EAT.</li>\n<li>VMC: <strong>8 km</strong> at and above 10,000 ft, else <strong>5 km</strong>; <strong>1,500 m / 1,000 ft</strong> from cloud; F/G low: clear of cloud, surface in sight.</li>\n<li>Steady red in flight = <strong>give way and keep circling</strong>; red flashes in flight = <strong>aerodrome unsafe</strong>; red pyro = <strong>do not land for the time being</strong>.</li>\n<li>Two yellow diagonals = <strong>landing prohibited</strong>; one diagonal = <strong>special precautions</strong>.</li>\n<li>Aerobatics must <strong>finish by 2,000 ft AGL</strong>; not within <strong>2 NM</strong> of an aerodrome below <strong>6,000 ft</strong>.</li>\n<li>Interception: follow <strong>visual</strong> signals over other radio instructions; squawk <strong>7700</strong>; 121.5 MHz.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>"};